# ISA — Virgin Australia Talent Acquisition & Onboarding AI Initiative

## Problem

Virgin Australia has a BCG report identifying cost-saving potential in talent acquisition and onboarding, but the advice is generic. The function has no end-to-end candidate management platform: discrete systems cover fragments of the process and Excel spreadsheets bridge the gaps. Direct (crew, pilots — standardised, bulk, likely higher-turnover) and indirect (specialised, role-specific) hiring streams run on separate platforms for reasons nobody has interrogated. Nothing about the current state is baselined: volumes, costs, effort, and system inventory are all unknown. Without a structured discovery, any AI initiative here would be building on sand — and the sequencing question (digitise first vs AI-native platform) cannot even be posed properly.

## Vision

Nick walks into his first discovery conversations with a document that makes him the best-prepared person in the room: hypotheses that are falsifiable rather than opinions, questions that expose the real structure of the problem in one pass, and a data request list that TA can action immediately. When the BCG partner-level genericness is mentioned, this document is the visible contrast. The euphoric surprise: discovery designed so that "digitise then AI" vs "AI-native platform that collapses both steps" is decided by evidence, not assumption.

## Out of Scope

No solution recommendation, vendor selection, or business case in this phase — discovery designs the evidence collection that makes those possible later. No process re-design; current state is mapped, not fixed. No commitments to VA stakeholders (Nick presents; the document does not get sent anywhere by the DA). No fabrication of Virgin Australia internal facts — everything VA-specific that Nick flagged as uncertain stays flagged. No OneDrive markdown exports.

## Principles

- Evidence before opinion: every hypothesis names the evidence that would falsify it.
- Baseline before benefit: savings claims are meaningless without a measured current state.
- The spreadsheet is a symptom, not the disease: Excel marks where systems fail to meet the process.
- Segmentation is a design choice, not an accident to be undone: volume and specialist hiring may genuinely need different workflows on a shared platform layer.
- Buy-lean: HR tech is a mature market; custom builds must justify themselves against configuration of existing platforms.
- Recommendation first, then reasoning — every artefact is executive-ready (CEO/CIO/CHRO audience).

## Constraints

- Nick's position: Senior AI Engineer, CIO/AI team, reporting to Head of AI — discovery must be executable from that seat (internal interviews, data requests), not from a consultant's mandate.
- Australian English, no em dashes, markdown source stays in the workspace.
- All VA-specific claims Nick marked uncertain ("I don't know this for sure") are carried as hypotheses, never facts.
- External market facts (vendors, case studies, benchmarks) must come from verified research with real URLs.
- Timebox framing: discovery structured to produce a defensible read in weeks, not a months-long study.

## Goal

Produce, in `DiscoveryApproach.md`, a complete discovery-phase design for the VA TA + onboarding AI initiative — falsifiable hypotheses, a themed question bank mapped to those hypotheses, an actionable data collection register, a stakeholder map, a sequenced plan, and an evidence-based market/benchmark scan — such that Nick can start discovery interviews and data requests immediately.

